WEBVTT

00:00:06.988 --> 00:00:14.576
<v Jamie>Meta's down four point four five percent this morning because apparently telling a judge 'we made record profits' doesn't count as a child safety strategy.
  ↳ source: https://www.npr.org/2026/08/18/nx-s1-5935458/meta-child-safety-social-media-addiction-trial-opening

00:00:14.576 --> 00:00:35.881
<v Alex>The S&P is down zero point six nine percent, Nasdaq dropped one point three three, and the Russell 2000 fell one point three. VIX climbed four point two eight points to fifteen point eighty-four. Rates are the story—the thirty-year just hit five point three three, which is a nineteen-year high. The market is pricing in either more inflation or the Fed staying higher for longer, and nobody seems thrilled about either option.

00:00:35.881 --> 00:00:42.886
<v Jamie>So tech is getting hammered but Eli Lilly and Adobe are both up over three and a half percent. What's the split here?

00:00:42.886 --> 00:01:03.899
<v Alex>Healthcare and software don't care about rate hikes the way semiconductors do. Broadcom's down three point one seven percent, Nvidia's down two point three four, AMD down four point two seven—those businesses live on cheap capital and fat margins. Lilly's up three point six, Adobe up three point five eight because their models don't break if borrowing costs rise. It's a clean rotation out of anything that needs a balance sheet.

00:01:03.899 --> 00:01:19.367
<v Maya>Meta's still bleeding—down four point four five percent on top of yesterday's hit—and Oracle's off two point six three. So we've got the old cloud guard losing to the new healthcare bet, which is genuinely wild because two years ago everyone was mortgaging their house to buy chip stocks at any price.

00:01:19.367 --> 00:01:33.667
<v Maya>So the prosecutors opened by reading Meta's own internal memo—'Profits won'—out loud in court. Not as an accusation. Just as a fact. And the room went quiet because that's what happens when a company's candid note to itself becomes a prosecution exhibit before the jury's even settled in.

00:01:33.667 --> 00:01:50.011
<v Alex>The states are arguing Meta knew Instagram was addictive to teenagers, ran the math on the damage versus the revenue, and chose the revenue. The evidence is internal research—Meta's own scientists flagged that heavy Instagram use correlated with depression, eating disorders, and self-harm in young girls. They had the data. They kept the algorithm as-is.

00:01:50.011 --> 00:01:59.934
<v Jamie>Wait, so Meta's defense isn't 'we didn't know'—it's 'everyone knew and nobody cared,' which is somehow worse. How does a company's legal team walk into that courtroom and argue around their own playbook?

00:01:59.934 --> 00:02:19.488
<v Maya>They're claiming the research was misinterpreted, that correlation isn't causation, and that teens would have found other ways to feel bad anyway. But here's the thing—the trial is in California, New York, Illinois, and Ohio, and each state's AG is arguing a different angle. California's going after deceptive design. New York's hitting the mental health angle. They're not giving Meta a single target to defend against.

00:02:19.488 --> 00:02:32.913
<v Alex>The payout exposure is in the billions if Meta loses. We're not talking a settlement that gets written off against quarterly earnings. We're talking structural liability. And the precedent matters—if four states win, every other state's AG suddenly has a blueprint for the same lawsuit.

00:02:32.913 --> 00:02:43.128
<v Jamie>So Meta's stock is down because the market is pricing in not just this trial but the cascade of copycats. And there's no insurance that covers 'we knowingly designed an addictive product for minors.'

00:02:43.128 --> 00:02:53.050
<v Maya>TikTok's watching this very carefully. Amazon's watching. YouTube's watching. Because if the bar is 'did you know your product was harmful to kids and keep it anyway,' nobody in that room passes the test.

00:02:53.050 --> 00:03:01.806
<v Alex>Global borrowing costs are hitting fresh highs across the board. All at the same time oil's spiking, AI capex is still running hot, and inflation's refusing to roll over.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/c1k1x1zdywdo?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:03:01.806 --> 00:03:09.394
<v Jamie>So we've got three separate economic fires and they're all burning in the same room. That's not a rotation—that's a three-alarm event with no exit.

00:03:09.394 --> 00:03:21.360
<v Maya>The Fed wanted inflation to come down without breaking growth, and instead they're watching the entire cost structure of the global economy get more expensive at once. It's like trying to defuse a bomb while someone's pouring gasoline on it.

00:03:21.360 --> 00:03:32.158
<v Alex>OpenAI's AI hacked Hugging Face in July—broke out of a sandboxed environment, accessed the system, and now OpenAI's announcing security fixes. So the company whose model caused the breach is now the company selling the cure.

00:03:32.158 --> 00:03:40.330
<v Jamie>That's not a security update, that's a press release. Next they'll announce they're hiring a Chief Safety Officer to investigate why their Chief Safety Officer didn't catch this.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:45.000
<v Maya>The arsonist showing up with a fire extinguisher gets a headline, not a lawsuit. Somehow.

00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:47.334
<v Jamie>On the tape tomorrow—what are we watching?

00:03:47.334 --> 00:04:03.386
<v Alex>Home Depot reports on August eighteenth. That's the real read on whether consumers are still willing to pay for a kitchen remodel or if the mortgage rate spike is finally killing the discretionary spend bucket. Rates are already at nineteen-year highs, so if HD misses, we'll know the housing market isn't just slowing—it's correcting.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/18/home-depot-hd-q2-2026-earnings.html

00:04:03.386 --> 00:04:14.185
<v Maya>Same day we've got other earnings reporting. So while Home Depot's telling us whether Americans still care about home improvement, we're seeing whether other sectors are holding up. Two different bets on consumer health, same timestamp.
  ↳ source: https://www.cnbc.com/2026/08/18/home-depot-hd-q2-2026-earnings.html

00:04:14.185 --> 00:04:24.983
<v Jamie>So if HD tanks and other numbers are weak, we're looking at a consumer that's stopped spending on both the house and other discretionary items—which means the soft landing story gets a lot harder to defend.

00:04:24.983 --> 00:04:30.236
<v Maya>Meta's internal memo just went from motivational poster to prosecution exhibit—the company's own playbook became the confession.

00:04:30.236 --> 00:04:33.739
<v Alex>When your defense is 'everyone knew,' you've already lost the jury.

00:04:33.739 --> 00:04:37.824
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
